===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|*hutte}}, {{m|enm|hotte}}, from both {{inh|en|ang|hōd}} and {{cog|ang|hȳdan|t=to hide}} and influenced by {{der|en|xno|hute}} or {{m|xno|hutte}}, from {{der|en|frm|hutte}}, from {{der|en|fro|hute|t=hut}}, {{m|fro|hute|t=cottage}}, from {{der|en|goh|hutta|t=hut, cottage}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hudjǭ}}, {{m|gem-pro|*hudjō|t=hut}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)kewt-|t=to deck; cover; covering; skin}}.

Cognate with {{cog|de|Hütte|t=hut}}, {{cog|nl|hut|t=hut}}, {{cog|fy|hutte|t=hut}}, {{cog|stq|Hutte|t=hut}}, {{cog|da|hytte|t=hut}}, {{cog|nb|hytte|t=hut}}, {{cog|sv|hydda|t=hut}}. Related to {{m|en|hide}}.
[[File:NGR 2009-021909-D052 (5654697437).jpg|thumb|Thatched hut in Niger]]
[[File:Madeira (2694526786).jpg|thumb|Stone hut in Madeira]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A small, simple one-[[storey]] [[dwelling]] or [[shelter]], often with just one room, and generally built of [[readily]] available local materials.
#: {{ux|en|a [[thatch]]ed '''hut'''; a mud '''hut'''; a shepherd’s '''hut'''}}
#* '''1625''', {{w|Nicholas Breton}}, “An Untrained Souldiour” in ''Characters and Essayes,'' Aberdeen: Edward Raban, p.{{nbsp}}31,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A01470.0001.001]</sup>
#*: And in his '''Hut''', when hee to rest doth take him,
#*: Hee sleeps, till Drums or deadlie Pellets wake him.
#* '''1751''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, ''{{w|The Rambler}},'' No.{{nbsp}}186, 28{{nbsp}}December, 1751, Volume{{nbsp}}6, London: J. Payne and J. Bouquet, 1752, pp.{{nbsp}}108-109,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004772607.0001.006]</sup>
#*: {{...}} love, that extends his dominion wherever humanity can be found, perhaps exerts the same power in the ''Greenlander''’s '''hut''', as in the palaces of eastern monarchs.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Great Expectations|II|XX|341|text={{...}} I was a hired-out shepherd in a solitary '''hut''', not seeing no faces but faces of sheep till I half forgot [[what|wot]] men’s and women’s faces [[were|wos]] like,}}
#* {{RQ:Achebe Things Fall Apart|chapter=11|page=99|passage=There was an oil lamp in all the four '''huts''' on Okonkwo’s [[compound#Etymology_1|compound]], and each '''hut''' seen from the others looked like a soft eye of yellow half-light set in the solid massiveness of night.}}
# A small wooden [[shed]].
#: {{ux|en|a groundsman’s '''hut'''}}
# {{lb|en|agriculture|obsolete}} A small [[stack]] of [[grain]].<ref>''A Letter to the West Country Farmers, concerning the Difficulties and Management of a Bad Harvest,'' Paisley, 1773, p.{{nbsp}}33: “A hut of corn is a small clump or stack, resembling a hay quoil or rick; and consists of about forty, fifty, or more sheaves {{...}}”<sup>[https://archive.org/details/lettertowestcoun00pais/page/n37]</sup></ref>

