# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To provide (someone) with shelter in a hut.
#: {{ux|en|to '''hut''' troops in winter quarters}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1631|translator=w:Henry Hexham|title=The Art of Fortification|author=Samuel Marolois|location=Amsterdam|publisher=John Johnson|section=Part 2, Figure 124 & 125|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A07035.0001.001
|passage={{...}} commonly the Captaines, after their souldiers are '''hutted''', build Hutts in the place, where their tents stood,}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1803|author=w:Robert Charles Dallas|title=The History of the Maroons|location=London|publisher=Longman and Rees|section=Volume 1, Letter 6, p. 200|url=https://archive.org/details/historymaroonsf01dallgoog/page/n333
|passage={{...}} the [[site|scite]] of the New Town, where divisions of the 17th and 20th light dragoons had '''hutted''' themselves.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1850|author=w:Washington Irving|title=The Life of Washington|location=New York|publisher=John W. Lovell|volume=2|chapter=56|page=443|url=https://archive.org/details/lifeofgeorgewas3v4irvi/page/n181
|passage=His troops, '''hutted''' among the heights of Morristown, were half fed, half clothed, and inferior in number to the garrison of New York.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|intransitive}} To take shelter in a hut.
#* '''1653''', Newsletter sent from London to {{w|Edward Nicholas}} dated 17{{nbsp}}June, 1653, in {{w|William Dunn Macray}} (ed.), ''Calendar of the {{w|Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon|Clarendon}} State Papers,'' Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1869, Volume{{nbsp}}2, p.{{nbsp}}219,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/calendarclarend01routgoog/page/n230]</sup>
#*: Seven boatfuls of Dutch prisoners have been taken to Chelsea College, where they are to '''hut''' under the walls.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1778|author=William Gordon|title=The History of the Rise, Progress, and Establishment, of the Independence of the United States of America|location=London|section=Volume 3, Letter 1, p. 11|url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc1.31175035539793&view=1up&seq=31
|passage=He removed with the troops, on the 19th, to Valley-forge, where they '''hutted''', about sixteen miles from Philadelphia.}}
# {{lb|en|agriculture|obsolete|transitive}} To [[stack]] ([[sheaf|sheaves]] of [[grain]]).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1796|author=James Donaldson|title=Modern Agriculture; or, The Present State of Husbandry in Great Britain|volume=2|url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015065555347&view=1up&seq=437|page=417|location=Edinburgh
|passage=The method of endeavouring to save corn in bad harvests, by '''hutting''' it in the field, is often practised in the north and west of Scotland,}}

